    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d54">

                <pb facs="0157" n="911" xml:id="pg911"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/4055</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Cuban Minister</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">De Céspedes</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 165</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-07-25">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 25, 1917</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your note of July 16, 1917,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d54-f1"/> enclosing
                    a communication addressed to the Department of State of your Government by Senor
                        René Berndes, president of the J. F. [Berndes] Co., and
                    requesting to be informed whether, upon the retirement from the management of
                    the company of the German subject who is its vice president, it would be
                    stricken from the American black list.</p>
                <p>In reply I have the honor to inform you that the Government of the United States
                    has not issued any black list of firms in Cuba or in other countries.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank L. Polk</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
